1. Wings: All birds have wings, which are used for flight. This is a distinguishing feature of birds compared to many other mammals.
2. Feathers: Most birds have feathers covering their bodies and tails. They also often have unique patterns on their wings and tail that can be identified as bird feathers.
3. Beak: Birds usually have a beak, which is used for eating and making sounds. Some species of birds like penguins do not have beaks at all.
4. Ability to Fly: Many birds are capable of flying, although some mammals also have the ability to fly but cannot fly in the same way as birds.
5. Eggs: Birds lay eggs which can hatch into young birds. This is a distinguishing feature from other animals that do not lay eggs.
6. Behaviour: Birds are known for their behaviour, with some species showing unique behaviours such as songbirds chirping and parrots talking.
7. Tail: Some birds have tails that are used for balance and to control flight. This is a distinguishing feature from other animals which do not typically have tails.
8. Evolutionary Relationship: Birds are one of the oldest living organisms on earth, having evolved from earlier avian ancestors more than 200 million years ago.
9. Sparrow-like Beak Shape: Many birds have a beak that is similar to that of a sparrow or other small bird species. This is another distinguishing feature.
10. Ability to Fly and Eat Seeds: Some birds are able to fly and eat seeds, which is not common among all mammals. 

Please note that these are general characteristics shared by most birds, but some species may have unique features or characteristics that make them distinguishable from other animals.

